#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262905 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{f}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{tan}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{tan}t-\\operatorname{sec}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{tan}t-\\operatorname{sec}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262906 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(9-5)-1(6-3)-1(10-9) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-3-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262907 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(7,6,4),$ $B(8,5,9),$ $C(7,4,2),$ and $D(5,7,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(8\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+9\\mathbf{k})-(7\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,5⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(7\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(7\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(7\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-2))+(0\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-2))+5(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2-4-20 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-22|=22.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262908 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1-3)-3(2-1)-5(6-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2-3-25 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=-30\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$T$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(T)=-30$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262909 :question/problem "Find  $(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}t⟩$ and $u(t)=2\\sqrt{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n \n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{t}⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}⟨\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}4t\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2\\sqrt{t}⟩+⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}t\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.167em}5t\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}3\\sqrt{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262910 :question/problem "Find  $(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}1+t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}⟩$ and $u(t)=1-t.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & =(1-t)⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩-⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}1+t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-t,\\hspace{0.167em}1-t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t-2{t}^{2}⟩-⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}1+t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2t,\\hspace{0.167em}-2t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t-3{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262911 :question/problem "Find  $(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨4\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}t+{t}^{2}⟩$ and $u(t)=2t.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n \n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1+2t⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & =2t⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1+2t⟩+2⟨4\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}t+{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}2t+4{t}^{2}⟩+⟨8\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}2t+2{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}8{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}4t+6{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262912 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(5,1,4),$ $B(4,1,7),$ $C(3,1,5),$ and $D(2,3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1)-0+3((-2)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2-12 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-10|=10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262913 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$R$ is a singular matrix” and $det(R)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262914 :question/problem "Find  $(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨2{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}4\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2⟩$ and $u(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{-t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{4}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}⟨-2{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{4}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩+t⟨2{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}4\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩+⟨2t{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}4t\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t{e}^{-t}-{t}^{2}{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t+4t\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t{e}^{-t}(2-t),\\hspace{0.167em}2t(1+2\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262917 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(B)=12,$ find the value of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ y & 1\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ y & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1-y)-5\\cdot (2-2y) \\\\  & =12y-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $12,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12y-12 & =12 \\\\ 12y & =24 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262918 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand $det(C)=28,$ find the value of $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}q & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & q \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (q+25)-3\\cdot (-1)+5 \\\\  & =2q+58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $28,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q+58 & =28 \\\\ 2q & =-30 \\\\ q & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262919 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y) &=0 \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{sin}y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262920 :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $g(x,y)={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f∇g+g∇f.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}={y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =x{y}^{2}(-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2x{y}^{3}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262921 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y,z) & =\\frac{1}{xyz},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h∇f+f∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)] & =h(x,y,z)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)+f(x,y,z)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xyz}({y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{{x}^{2}yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xy{z}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}-\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262923 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{g}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{ln}2u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{tan}u⟩$ and $u(t)={t}^{2},$ then $(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}2u),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{tan}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}u⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}(u(t)),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u(t)},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}(u(t))⟩\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}({t}^{2})⟩\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =⟨2t\\operatorname{cos}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}({t}^{2})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262924 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{f}(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ {u}^{2} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)={e}^{t}$, then $(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(6)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{f}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u(t) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2({e}^{t}) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262925 :question/problem "Find $(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}$ given that  $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}t⟩$ and $v(t)=2t.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}=v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩ \\\\ {v}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & =v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t) \\\\  & =2t⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩+2⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩+⟨2\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{ln}t+1,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262926 :question/problem "Find  $(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{3}⟩$ and $u(t)={t}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(u\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{2}⟩+3{t}^{2}⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{5}⟩+⟨3{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}12{t}^{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262930 :question/problem "Find $(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}$ given that  $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{2t}⟩$ and $v(t)={e}^{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}=v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t+1}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\ {v}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & =v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{2t}⟩+{e}^{t}⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{3t}⟩+⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}1,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{3t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}0,\\hspace{0.167em}3{e}^{3t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262933 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x} & =c \\\\ x+{y}^{2} & =cx \\\\ x(1-c)+{y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, “$c=-1$ gives a line” is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curves is $x=-{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, “$c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin” is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, “$c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin” is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is “$c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin”; “$c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262934 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-1){y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis. So, “$c=0$ gives a parabola” is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $x=0,$ which is the $y$-axis. So, “$c=1$ gives an ellipse” is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$  then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2\\,⟹\\,2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, the only real solution to this equation is  $(x,y)=(0,0),$ which is not in the domain of $f(x,y)$ because it would result in division by zero. Thus, “$c=2$ gives a hyperbola” is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262935 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =c \\\\ y & =cx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is the line $y=-x,$ excluding the origin. So, “$c=-1$ gives a hyperbola” is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, “$c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, excluding the origin” is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin. So, “$c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin” is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is “$c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262943 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-3,3].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-3,3].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =c \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\frac{c}{3}+2\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that the equations $x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\frac{c}{3}+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $y.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-3,3]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of vertical lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cdd8cec2d30ff2956d5210a49036c17d99781d0b62c0b0f42f97d0575c4d80f6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262944 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=-x-y+2?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nnotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider $c$-level curves for any real value of $c.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ -x-y+2 & =c \\\\ y & =-x+2-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of lines with slope of $-1.$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/e2/e20175ef9ac473a621a67d524e60cacc6e9a40ba30267fe81b4e6a093c780c28.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265711 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(0,-1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(0,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{0+1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265712 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(1)-2(-2)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265713 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+1(2)}{(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x+y)}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x+y)}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y} & =\\frac{2}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265714 :question/problem "Determine $$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{2}^{3}-3({2}^{2})(2)+3(2)({2}^{2})-{2}^{3}}{{2}^{2}-2(2)(2)+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-24+24-8}{4-8+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)^{3}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)(x-y)^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)^{2}}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}(x-y) & =2-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265715 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x-y)}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}}{x\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =\\frac{-1+(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265716 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}+0-0}{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0+0-0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{x-y+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(x-y)(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x-y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =1+\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =1+0+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265717 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}-1}{({1}^{2})(2+(-1))-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{(x\\sqrt{y+z}-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)}(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265718 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & =\\frac{1+0-{1}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{(\\sqrt{x+y}-z)(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{x+y-{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x+y-{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x+y-{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z) & =\\sqrt{1+0}+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265719 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}-3}{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9-9}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265721 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is also true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265722 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265723 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}x=0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265823 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1),$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2(0)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\  & =(-1)i+0\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-0⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265826 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2}$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-4y)\\,i+(10y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,i+(10(-1)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265852 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}.$"}]}
